import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const groupCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'Set: hours on a clock {0, 1, …, 11}', note: 'Take addition "mod 12": 9 + 5 = 2, because 14 wraps to 2. Ordinary arithmetic, wrapped around.' },
    { math: 'Closure: a + b stays in the set', note: 'Axiom 1 — adding two clock hours never leaves the clock.' },
    { math: 'Identity: 0 changes nothing', note: 'Axiom 2 — there is an element that acts like "doing nothing".' },
    { math: 'Inverses: every a has −a', note: 'Axiom 3 — every shift can be undone. 5 + 7 = 0 (mod 12). These three (plus associativity) make it a group.' },
    { math: 'Groups everywhere: rotations, integers, symmetries', note: 'Any system with a reversible, combinable operation is a group — and its theorems apply. That universality is why "abstract" algebra isn\'t abstract at all.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/expert/abstract-algebra">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Abstract algebra does something that looks like madness and turns out to be profound: it
    <strong>forgets the objects and keeps the rules</strong>. Add numbers, add clock-hours,
    combine rotations, flip a Rubik's cube — all different activities, all obeying the same
    pattern of axioms. Prove a theorem once about the pattern, and it holds for every system
    that shares it. The pattern with combine-identity-inverse is called a <strong>group</strong>.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="clock arithmetic — a complete group, checked axiom by axiom" widget="equationStep" config={groupCfg} />

  <h2>The algebraic zoo</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Groups</strong> — one reversible operation. Symmetry is a group: the ways you can rotate a square (0°, 90°, 180°, 270°, flips) form one, and chemistry's molecular symmetry and particle physics' gauge symmetries are the same mathematics.</li>
    <li><strong>Rings</strong> — two operations (add and multiply) that cooperate: integers, polynomials, matrices.</li>
    <li><strong>Fields</strong> — rings where division works: rationals, reals, complex numbers, and the finite fields inside every error-correcting code and AES encryption.</li>
    <li><strong>Lagrange's theorem:</strong> the size of any subgroup divides the size of the group — a counting fact with no formula, yet decisive in everything from card shuffles to why quintic equations have no radical solution (Galois, age 20, 1832).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why experts care</h2>
  <p>
    Algebra is compression: instead of proving things about integers, matrices, permutations and
    symmetries separately, prove them once for groups and rings. When a physicist says a particle
    "transforms under SU(3)", or a cryptographer says a curve is "the group E(𝔽ₚ)", they are
    invoking theorems that were proven once, here.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Artin, Algebra; Dummit & Foote, Abstract Algebra; Judson, Abstract Algebra: Theory and Applications (open text).</p>
</TopicPage>
